Good. Now I've changed the approach to:1. Use `setEndIconMode(END_ICON_NONE)` to disable the library's built-in toggle
2. Manually set the end icon drawable in `togglePasswordVisibility()`3. Both click listeners call the same `togglePasswordVisibility()` method

This ensures:
- No conflict between library behavior and our custom behavior- We fully control the end icon drawable and text visibility
- Both fields toggle together

Now the `updateLayout()` method's call to `togglePasswordVisibility()` will set up the initial hidden state. Let me verify the `togglePasswordVisibility()` method looks correct:

```java
private void togglePasswordVisibility() {
    showPassword = !showPassword;
    if (showPassword) {
        passwordInput.setTransformationMethod(null);
        passwordConfirm.setTransformationMethod(null);
        passwordLayout.setEndIconDrawable(R.drawable.ic_visibility_visible);        passwordConfirmLayout.setEndIconDrawable(R.drawable.ic_visibility_visible);
    } else {
        passwordInput.setTransformationMethod(new PasswordTransformationMethod());
        passwordConfirm.setTransformationMethod(new PasswordTransformationMethod());
        passwordLayout.setEndIconDrawable(R.drawable.ic_visibility_invisible);
        passwordConfirmLayout.setEndIconDrawable(R.drawable.ic_visibility_invisible);
    }
}```

This looks correct. When called:
- If `showPassword` was false → becomes true → fields become visible, icons change to visible- If `showPassword` was true → becomes false → fields become hidden, icons change to invisible

Initial flow:1. `onBindDialogView` → `showPassword = false`
2. `onBindDialogView` → `togglePasswordVisibility()` → `showPassword = true` → fields become visible, icons set to visible

But that's wrong! The fields should start hidden! The problem is that `onBindDialogView` already calls `togglePasswordVisibility()` to initialize, so when `updateLayout()` calls it again, it will flip back to hidden. I need to either skip the initialization call in `onBindDialogView` or adjust the logic so the first call properly sets the initial state. I'm going in circles here—each approach has a flaw. The cleanest solution is to skip the toggle method entirely during setup and just directly apply the hidden state to both password fields. I need to remove the toggle call from the setup section and replace it with direct initialization of both password fields to their hidden state.

